#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054500 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054757 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be393dbe-03cc-5259-ba28-8f865537c57a" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing Two Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "04b471e2-edcd-5f01-8677-e93ddc547a0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-18927" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{3} + 1$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{3} + 1$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\sqrt{3} + 1} {\\sqrt{3} + 1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {\\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 - 1^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {3 - 1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {2\\left(\\sqrt 3 + 1\\right)} {2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt 3 + 1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054501 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eae5693c-a37e-58f4-8793-0875ef8e0fb8" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing Two Surds"}] :question/id #uuid "0e811568-769f-5e18-9c6c-5594c18330b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-18931" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{6}{\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{6}}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{6}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{6}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}} &=  \\\\\n(6(\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}))/((\\sqrt{10})^{2} - (\\sqrt{6})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n(6(\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}))/(10 - 6) &=  \\\\\n(6(\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}))/(4) &=  \\\\\n&= (3(\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}))/(2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054502 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "203f17e9-3efb-5637-ac02-2c31e5a8a632" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing Surds in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "5a664cc0-30ca-5097-9505-0755963b0f1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-18932" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2} + 1}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2} - 1$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2} - 1$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2} + 1} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2} + 1} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2} - 1}{\\sqrt{2} - 1} &=  \\\\\n((2 - \\sqrt{2})(\\sqrt{2} - 1))/((\\sqrt{2} + 1)(\\sqrt{2} - 1)) &=  \\\\\n(2\\sqrt{2} - 2 - (\\sqrt{2})^{2} + \\sqrt{2})/((\\sqrt{2})^{2} - (1)^{2}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2\\sqrt{2} - 2 - 2 + \\sqrt{2}}{2 - 1} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2\\sqrt{2} - 4 + \\sqrt{2}}{1} &=  \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{2} - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
